  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Temporary Protected Status (TPS) does not automatically lead to a green card, but holders can adjust status if they meet eligibility requirements like family sponsorship, employment-based sponsorship, or a lawful initial entry. TPS provides a safe harbor to pursue other immigration avenues, such as applying for permanent residence if a visa is available. Key Pathways from TPS to Green Card Family-Based Sponsorship: If you have an immediate relative (spouse, parent, or unmarried child under 21) who is a U.S. citizen. Employment-Based Green Card: Sponsorship by an employer through Form I-140. Lawful Admission Requirement: Generally, for \"adjustment of status\" (applying for a green card within the U.S.), you must have initially entered the U.S. legally (with inspection/visa), say Khalique Law PLLC and USCIS. Travel and Re-entry: A previously popular route to \"lawful admission\" status was traveling on Advance Parole and re-entering. While the Supreme Court barred this for those who initially entered illegally (Sanchez v. Mayorkas), it does not apply to those who initially entered with a visa and overstayed, notes Immigrant Legal Defense. Important Considerations Status Maintenance: You must maintain your TPS status through re-registration. Asylum: TPS holders may be able to apply for asylum if they meet the criteria. Legal Advice: Immigration laws are complex; consulting a qualified immigration attorney is strongly recommended to review specific cases.",
